{
  "question": "In the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system (RAAS), which mechanism most directly explains how angiotensin II contributes to restoration of arterial blood pressure during decreased renal perfusion?",
  "options": {
    "A": "It inhibits aldosterone secretion, increasing sodium excretion and reducing plasma volume.",
    "B": "It causes systemic arteriolar vasoconstriction and stimulates aldosterone release, promoting renal sodium and water retention.",
    "C": "It directly suppresses sympathetic nervous system activity, reducing cardiac output.",
    "D": "It increases atrial natriuretic peptide release, enhancing diuresis and natriuresis."
  },
  "correct_answer": "B",
  "explanation": "Decreased renal perfusion stimulates renin release from juxtaglomerular cells. Renin promotes formation of angiotensin I, which is converted by ACE to angiotensin II. Angiotensin II increases arterial pressure by causing systemic arteriolar vasoconstriction and by stimulating aldosterone secretion from the adrenal cortex, leading to increased renal sodium and water retention."
}

