{
  "Question": "In a radioligand binding assay, Drug A and Drug B bind reversibly to the same receptor population. Drug A has a dissociation constant (Kd) of 2 nM, while Drug B has a Kd of 50 nM. Functional assays show that Drug A produces a maximal response equal to the endogenous ligand, whereas Drug B binds the receptor but produces no response and shifts the concentration-response curve of Drug A to the right without reducing its maximal effect. Which statement best describes these drugs?",
  "Options": {
    "A": "Drug A has lower affinity than Drug B and is a partial agonist; Drug B is a noncompetitive antagonist.",
    "B": "Drug A has higher affinity than Drug B and is a full agonist; Drug B is a competitive antagonist with zero intrinsic activity.",
    "C": "Drug A has higher efficacy but lower affinity than Drug B; Drug B is an irreversible antagonist.",
    "D": "Drug A and Drug B have identical affinity, but Drug A has greater potency; Drug B is a physiological antagonist.",
    "E": "Drug A has lower Kd and therefore lower receptor affinity; Drug B has high intrinsic activity but no efficacy."
  },
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Explanation": "A lower Kd indicates higher binding affinity because Kd is inversely related to receptor affinity. Therefore, Drug A, with a Kd of 2 nM, has higher affinity than Drug B, which has a Kd of 50 nM. Because Drug A produces the same maximal response as the endogenous ligand, it is a full agonist. Drug B binds the receptor but produces no response, indicating zero intrinsic activity. Its ability to shift the Drug A concentration-response curve to the right without reducing maximal response is characteristic of a reversible competitive antagonist."
}

