      "Aspirin is unique because it irreversibly inhibits COX, producing a prolonged antiplatelet effect."
    ],
    "Homework or Extended Learning": [
      "Compare ibuprofen, aspirin, naproxen, and celecoxib in terms of COX selectivity, duration of action, common uses, and major adverse effects.",
      "Prepare a brief patient education note explaining how to use over-the-counter NSAIDs safely.",
      "Read about the difference between NSAIDs and corticosteroids in the arachidonic acid pathway."
    ],
    "Common Misconceptions and Corrections": [
      {
        "Misconception": "All NSAIDs are the same.",
        "Correction": "NSAIDs share COX inhibition but differ in COX selectivity, duration, potency, adverse effect profile, and clinical use."
      },
      {
        "Misconception": "COX-2 selective drugs have no adverse effects.",
        "Correction": "COX-2 selective inhibitors may reduce gastric toxicity but can increase cardiovascular risk in certain patients."
      },
      {
        "Misconception": "NSAIDs cure the underlying cause of inflammation.",
        "Correction": "NSAIDs mainly reduce inflammatory mediators and symptoms; they do not necessarily remove the underlying cause."
      },
      {
        "Misconception": "Aspirin's effect on platelets disappears when the drug leaves the blood.",
        "Correction": "Aspirin irreversibly inhibits platelet COX-1, so its antiplatelet effect lasts for the lifespan of the platelet."
      }
    ]
  }
}

