        "Develop awareness of the dynamic nature of tumor evolution during therapy.",
        "Appreciate the need for multidisciplinary collaboration in managing resistant cancers."
      ]
    },
    "key_points": [
      {
        "topic": "Definition and classification of drug resistance",
        "details": [
          "Intrinsic resistance: tumor cells are resistant before treatment begins.",
          "Acquired resistance: tumor cells initially respond but later become resistant during or after therapy.",
          "Cross-resistance: resistance to one drug leads to resistance to other drugs, often due to shared mechanisms."
        ]
      },
      {
        "topic": "Genetic mechanisms",
        "details": [
          "Mutation of drug targets, such as EGFR T790M mutation after first-generation EGFR inhibitors.",
          "Gene amplification, such as HER2 or MET amplification.",
          "Activation of alternative signaling pathways that bypass the inhibited target.",
          "Defects in apoptosis-related genes, such as TP53 mutations."
        ]
      },
      {
        "topic": "Epigenetic mechanisms",
        "details": [
          "DNA methylation changes can silence tumor suppressor genes.",
          "Histone modification can alter gene expression related to survival and resistance.",
          "Non-coding RNAs, including microRNAs and long non-coding RNAs, may regulate drug response."
        ]
      },
      {
        "topic": "Drug transport and metabolism",
        "details": [
          "Increased drug efflux through ATP-binding cassette transporters, such as P-glycoprotein/MDR1.",
          "Reduced drug uptake through downregulation of transporters.",
          "Enhanced drug detoxification by enzymes such as glutathione S-transferases.",
          "Altered prodrug activation, leading to reduced active drug levels."
        ]
      },
      {
        "topic": "DNA damage repair and cell death resistance",
        "details": [
          "Enhanced DNA repair can reduce the effect of chemotherapy and radiotherapy.",
          "Defects in apoptotic signaling allow cancer cells to survive treatment.",
          "Autophagy may protect tumor cells under therapeutic stress."
        ]
      },
      {
        "topic": "Tumor microenvironment",
        "details": [
          "Hypoxia can reduce drug sensitivity and promote aggressive phenotypes.",
          "Cancer-associated fibroblasts and immune cells can secrete protective cytokines.",
          "Poor vascularization can limit drug delivery.",
          "Extracellular matrix remodeling can physically restrict drug penetration."
        ]
      },
