  "proof": [
    {
      "step": 1,
      "idea": "Model the sorting algorithm as a binary decision tree.",
      "explanation": "In a comparison-based sorting algorithm, the only way to gain information about the input is by comparing two elements. Each comparison has two possible outcomes, such as a_i < a_j or a_i > a_j, since all elements are distinct. Therefore, the algorithm can be represented as a binary decision tree."
    },
    {
      "step": 2,
      "idea": "Each possible input order must lead to a different leaf.",
      "explanation": "There are n! possible permutations of n distinct elements. To correctly sort the input, the algorithm must distinguish between all these n! possible orderings. Hence, the decision tree must have at least n! leaves."
    },
    {
      "step": 3,
      "idea": "Relate the number of leaves to the height of the tree.",
      "explanation": "A binary tree of height h has at most 2^h leaves. Since the decision tree must have at least n! leaves, we must have 2^h >= n!."
    },
    {
      "step": 4,
      "idea": "Take logarithms.",
      "explanation": "From 2^h >= n!, we get h >= log_2(n!). The height h represents the worst-case number of comparisons made by the sorting algorithm."
    },
    {
      "step": 5,
      "idea": "Estimate log(n!).",
      "explanation": "Using the fact that n! >= (n/2)^(n/2), we get log_2(n!) >= log_2((n/2)^(n/2)) = (n/2) log_2(n/2). This is Ω(n log n)."
    }
  ],
  "conclusion": "Any comparison-based sorting algorithm must make at least Ω(n log n) comparisons in the worst case to sort n distinct elements.",
  "key_result": "The lower bound for comparison-based sorting is Ω(n log n)."
