    "key_points": [
      {
        "concept": "Consistency",
        "explanation": "Every read receives the most recent write or an error. All nodes appear to have the same data at the same time."
      },
      {
        "concept": "Availability",
        "explanation": "Every request receives a response, even if some nodes are unavailable or cannot communicate with each other."
      },
      {
        "concept": "Partition Tolerance",
        "explanation": "The system continues operating despite network failures that prevent nodes from communicating."
      }
    ],
    "cap_theorem_explanation": "In a distributed system, network partitions are unavoidable in practice. When a partition occurs, some nodes cannot communicate with others. At that point, the system must make a choice: preserve consistency by refusing some requests until nodes can synchronize, or preserve availability by allowing requests to continue even though different nodes may temporarily hold different data. Therefore, a system cannot guarantee consistency, availability, and partition tolerance all at the same time during a partition.",
