      "example": "An e-commerce shopping cart system",
      "priority": "Availability over consistency",
      "reason": "It is often more important that users can continue adding items to their carts and placing orders, even if some replicas temporarily have outdated information.",
      "description": "Suppose a network partition separates two data centers. If the system prioritizes availability, both data centers continue accepting cart updates. A user might add an item in one region while inventory data in another region has not yet been updated."
    },
    "implications": [
      "Users experience fewer service interruptions because the system continues responding to requests.",
      "Data may become temporarily inconsistent across replicas.",
      "Conflicts may occur, such as two users purchasing the last available item.",
      "The system must use conflict resolution techniques, such as eventual consistency, reconciliation, timestamps, version vectors, or compensating actions.",
      "After the partition is resolved, replicas synchronize and the system attempts to reach a consistent state."
    ],
    "conclusion": "The CAP theorem does not mean distributed systems are always missing one property. Instead, it means that during a network partition, a system must choose between consistency and availability. Systems that prioritize availability accept temporary inconsistency in exchange for continued operation."
